\value{
An object of class \code{"pigauto_fit"}.

The print method returns \code{x} invisibly.
}
\description{
Fits a phylogenetic baseline, with an optional attention-based graph
neural network (GNN) contribution. The GNN is disabled by default
(\code{gnn = FALSE}). Baseline routes depend on trait type, prediction
settings, and fit eligibility: they include continuous-family Brownian
motion, threshold/liability joint fits, categorical one-vs-rest fits,
label propagation, and component-wise BM on transformed compositions.
See \code{\link{fit_baseline}} for route eligibility and fallbacks.
All eight trait types share a latent representation. The internal torch
class \code{ResidualPhyloDAE} refers to residual skip connections;
its output is a full prediction, not a statistical residual.
}
\details{
\strong{Blend formulation:}
The neural blend is \eqn{\hat{x} = (1-r)\mu + r\delta}, where \eqn{\mu}
is the baseline, \eqn{\delta} is the model's direct prediction, and
\eqn{r = \sigma(\rho) \times \mathrm{cap}} is a per-column gate. A zero
neural weight returns the baseline only when no mean or covariate shortcut
contributes; validation calibration does not guarantee target-set error.

\strong{Training objective} (per epoch):
\enumerate{
\item A random subset of observed cells is corrupted with a learnable
mask token.
\item The model predicts \eqn{\delta} from graph context.
\item On randomly masked training cells, the objective combines the
type-specific loss for \eqn{\delta + fixed_effect}, 0.1 times the
type-specific loss for the blended prediction plus fixed effect,
\code{lambda_shrink} times MSE(\eqn{\delta - \mu}), and
\code{lambda_gate} times MSE(\eqn{r}).
}

The gate penalty on \eqn{r} is necessary because when \eqn{\delta =
\mu} (the BM-optimal solution for observed cells), the reconstruction
and shrinkage losses both equal zero regardless of \eqn{r}, leaving no
gradient to close the gate.  The explicit penalty ensures gates default
toward zero when the GNN correction provides no benefit.

\strong{Type-specific losses:}
\describe{
\item{continuous, count, ordinal, and proportion}{MSE on the latent scale}
\item{multi-proportion}{MSE in centred log-ratio (CLR) space}
\item{binary}{binary cross-entropy with logits}
\item{zero-inflated count}{binary cross-entropy for the nonzero gate,
plus MSE for the conditional magnitude}
\item{categorical}{cross-entropy over the K class logits}
}
}
\section{Calibration at small n}{


Conformal bounds use the empirical \code{(1 - alpha)} quantile of
\eqn{|y - \hat y|} on held-out validation cells. They are nominal
held-out diagnostics and do not certify package-wide coverage.

Small validation sets make both the residual quantile and gate selection
unstable. The \code{min_val_cells} warning identifies traits where this is
especially relevant. \code{conformal_split_val = TRUE} can separate gate
selection from residual estimation for eligible columns, but it does not
remove the small-sample limitation. Use \code{cross_validate()} on the
intended data and report the resulting held-out diagnostics rather than
treating an interval target as certified.
}

\section{Printing}{

Prints configuration, losses, gates and signal summaries. A printed signal label does not verify that a mean fallback was enabled. The \code{...} argument is currently unused.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
data(avonet300, tree300)
tree <- ape::keep.tip(tree300, tree300$tip.label[seq_len(30L)])
traits <- avonet300[match(tree$tip.label, avonet300$Species_Key),
                     c("Mass", "Wing.Length"), drop = FALSE]
rownames(traits) <- tree$tip.label
data <- preprocess_traits(traits, tree)
splits <- make_missing_splits(data$X_scaled, trait_map = data$trait_map,
                              seed = 1)
fit <- fit_pigauto(data, tree, splits = splits, epochs = 5L,
                   verbose = FALSE, seed = 1)
}
}
